From 2afd8d363f1a939977b25e4f458989a265650680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler South <tsouth2@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:01:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Give each display its own synced mode

Display sync applied every display's EDID mode through Omarchy's catch-all monitor rule, one after the other, so with two displays both outputs ended at the last display's mode. The first output no longer matched its window, and QEMU recreated the window of each output whose mode changed. The launcher put the recreated window back at its own size, which changed its EDID and started the next round, so the outputs flipped between the two sizes until the start timed out on a black wallpaper.

With several displays each output now gets its own rule. Hyprland cannot list its monitor rules, so when Hyprland starts or reloads, display sync runs the config Hyprland loads under Lua with hl.monitor recording, through the modules it requires on the search path Hyprland reports, and keeps the answer for the resizes that follow. Every output a rule there names, by connector or desc:, is left alone. The run fails closed: reading Hyprland's state, waiting for events, Omarchy helpers that check things, commands, file writes, a config that does not end, or a catch-all value that is not plain data leave every output alone.

A new rule for one output starts from Hyprland's defaults, so it carries every other field of the catch-all, except a mirror of that output itself. A mode or scale saved in the display panel of newer Omarchy, in ~/.local/state/omarchy/monitors/settings, stays. An output left with its own rule after the other displays go away follows its window with only the fields display sync set there.

One display keeps the catch-all rule and the recorded mode for reloads. Revision 76 delivers the script to existing disks.
